-        "markdown": "# Singapore Refined Petroleum Output Signals Energy-Cycle Exposure, but Momentum Weakened in 2025\n\nZNBW assesses Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing data as a useful signal of energy-cycle exposure rather than a standalone indicator of domestic demand. The movement points to sensitivity around fuel demand, travel activity, refining margins, trade flows, and operational factors in the refining complex.\n\nThe strongest verified near-term data point is that 2025 output reached **38344** million dollars, with latest year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**. That places the sector in a weaker momentum position even though the longer-period series remains above its 2016 level.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- [The main signal](#znbw-assessment) is cyclical exposure: refined petroleum output appears tied to global energy demand and refining-cycle conditions rather than a simple local production trend.\n- [The data shows](#what-the-data-shows) 10-year growth of **43.11%**, below total manufacturing growth of **77.56%**, implying relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points**.\n- [Sector ranking matters](#what-the-historical-evidence-shows): the supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6** major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth, requiring editorial review because the source series used here is refined petroleum products.\n- [Operators should watch](#what-operators-should-watch-next) fuel demand, travel recovery, refining margins, and refinery operating conditions as practical indicators for near-term exposure.\n\n## ZNBW Assessment\n\nThe available evidence suggests that Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing output is primarily being shaped by the **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle** mechanism. Confidence is **medium**, with the signal classified as an **emerging pattern** and a volatile pattern in the supplied assessment. The business relevance is strongest for petroleum, refining, energy, logistics, and trade-exposed operators. The main limitation is that the supplied mandatory findings refer to **Precision Engineering**, while the source series and graph package refer to **Refined Petroleum Products**; this label inconsistency requires human editorial review before publication.\n\n## What Happened\n\nSingapore’s refined petroleum products output weakened in the latest year after a period marked by large swings. The latest available annual value for 2025 is **38344** million dollars, with year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**.\n\nThis should be interpreted as a momentum warning rather than as evidence of structural collapse. The 10-year series remains higher than in 2016, but the pattern is volatile and appears consistent with exposure to global fuel demand, refining economics, and operating-cycle factors.\n\n[GRAPH: Singapore refined petroleum products output, 2016-2025]\n\n## What the Data Shows\n\nThe supplied intelligence records 10-year growth of **43.11%** for the relevant sector series versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing. That implies relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points** compared with total manufacturing.\n\nThis relative underperformance is important because total manufacturing expanded more strongly over the period. In business terms, refined petroleum-related manufacturing did not keep pace with the broader manufacturing base, even though the level of output remained above its 2016 value.\n\n[GRAPH: Indexed trend comparison of refined petroleum products output versus total manufacturing, 2016-2025]\n\nThe supplied manufacturing-share finding states that **Precision Engineering's manufacturing share increased from 10.35% in 2016 to 8.34% in 2025, a gain of -2.01 percentage points**. Numerically, a move from **10.35%** to **8.34%** with a **-2.01** percentage-point change indicates a decline in share; the wording of “increased” and “gain” should therefore be treated as a data-label or drafting issue requiring review.\n\n## What Explains the