-      "markdown": "# What Transport Engineering Reveals About Singapore’s Aviation and Marine Recovery\n\nZNBW’s assessment is that Singapore’s transport engineering signal is best read as a recovery-linked industrial cycle rather than a standalone manufacturing movement. The available evidence points to aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles as the main business mechanisms behind the sector’s recent strength.\n\nThe strongest verified signal is that the attached transport-equipment output series reached **33614.4** in **2025**, with latest year-on-year growth of **31.49%**. Over the longer period, the supplied intelligence also shows the sector’s 10-year performance running ahead of total manufacturing, which makes the movement relevant for operators exposed to aerospace, marine engineering, transport engineering, and MRO demand.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- [The transport engineering signal is consistent with recovery in aviation and marine activity](#znbw-assessment), with confidence assessed as high but still subject to human editorial review.\n- [The data shows a stronger 10-year trajectory than total manufacturing](#what-the-data-shows): the supplied intelligence records **113.54%** growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, an outperformance of **35.98 percentage points**.\n- [Sector positioning is meaningful but not dominant](#what-the-historical-evidence-shows): the supplied mandatory intelligence states a **#3 out of 6** rank by 10-year growth, while the article intelligence also records a latest year-on-year growth rank of **#2 out of 6**.\n- [Manufacturing share has widened](#what-the-data-shows), increasing from **6.08%** in **2016** to **7.32%** in **2025**, a gain of **1.23 percentage points**.\n- [Businesses should watch whether recovery-linked demand persists](#what-operators-should-watch-next), especially in MRO, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles.\n\n## ZNBW Assessment\n\nZNBW assesses the transport engineering movement as a **recovering expansion** driven primarily by aviation and marine recovery mechanisms, including aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles. Confidence is **high**, supported by an established-pattern assessment in the supplied intelligence, but the article remains subject to **human review** because publication approval has not been granted and because the supplied mandatory findings use the label “Precision Engineering” while the attached source series is “Other Transport Equipment.”\n\n## What happened\n\nSingapore’s transport engineering-linked output strengthened materially in the latest data period. The attached source series, **Other Transport Equipment**, reached **33614.4** in **2025**, with latest year-on-year growth of **31.49%**.\n\nThat movement matters because the transport-equipment category is closely aligned with business cycles in aerospace, marine engineering, and maintenance-related activity. For companies exposed to aircraft servicing, fleet support, ship repair, marine systems, or related engineering capacity, the data points to a stronger operating backdrop than was visible during the earlier post-2020 period.\n\n[GRAPH: Singapore other transport equipment output trend, 2016–2025]\n\nThe annual path shows a decline into 2020, followed by a recovery phase from 2021 onward. Output rose from **13592.3** in 2020 to **14812.2** in 2021, **18147.8** in 2022, **21571.0** in 2023, **25563.6** in 2024, and **33614.4** in 2025.\n\n## What the data shows\n\nThe supplied article intelligence indicates that the transport-equipment signal has outpaced total manufacturing over the 10-year period. The mandatory intelligence states that **Precision Engineering grew 113.54% over 10 years versus 77.56% for total manufacturing, an outperformance of 35.98 percentage points**. In the attached source data, those same 10-year growth values correspond to the **Other Transport Equipment** row and the **Total Manufacturing** row.\n\n[GRAPH: Indexed 10-year output trend for other transport equipment and total manufacturing, 2016–2025]\n\nThis relative performance is commercially relevant because it shows that the sector did more than recover in line with the broader manufacturing base. Total manufacturing output rose from **258788.4** in 2016 to **459504.1** in 2025, while other transport equipment rose from **15741.5** to **33614.4** over the same period.\n\nThe manufacturing share change also points to a larger footprint within the manufacturing base. The supplied mandatory intelligence states that **Precision Engineering’s manufacturing share increased from 6.08% in 2016 to 7.32% in 2025, a gain of 1.23 percentage points**. Using the attached transport-equipment series and total manufacturing series, this share movement is consistent with the reported values.\n\n## What explains the movement\n\nThe available evidence points to a transport engineering cycle shaped by aviation and marine recovery rather than by a single isolated driver. The article context identifies aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles as the relevant mechanisms.\n\nFor aerospace and MRO operators, stronger transport-equipment output can be consistent with higher aircraft servicing, maintenance, and engineering activity as air travel and fleet utilisation recover. For marine engineering and ship repair operators, the same category may reflect demand linked to vessel maintenance, offshore activity, and regional logistics requirements.\n\nThe data should not be interpreted as a detailed breakdown of aviation versus marine contribution. The source row is an aggregate manufacturing category, so it supports a sector-level signal rather than a full decomposition of sub-sector drivers.\n\n## What the historical evidence shows\n\nThe historical profile is consistent with a recovery followed by expansion. Output fell from **16690.5** in 2019 to **13592.3** in 2020, then moved upward across the subsequent periods in the attached annual data.\n\nThe supplied intelligence classifies the trend as **recovering_expansion**. It also assigns the pattern a high signal strength, with a confidence tier described as an **established_pattern** and a stability assessment described as a **stable_pattern**. The recurrence assessment is **partially_recurring**, which suggests the signal has repeated enough to be meaningful but should still be monitored rather than treated as permanent.\n\nSector ranking also supports a measured interpretation. The mandatory intelligence states that **Precision Engineering ranked #3 out of 6 major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth**. The article intelligence further records a latest year-on-year growth rank of **#2 out of 6**. This places the sector among stronger manufacturing performers, but not as the top-ranked 10-year growth category.\n\n## What operators should watch next\n\nOperators should watch whether the latest strength translates into durable order flow, capacity utilisation, and maintenance demand. The most relevant watch items are:\n\n- **Aviation maintenance demand:** whether aircraft servicing and MRO workloads remain supportive.\n- **Marine engineering and ship repair activity:** whether fleet maintenance and offshore-related demand continue to support output.\n- **Manufacturing-relative performance:** whether the sector continues to outpace total manufacturing after the strong 2025 year-on-year reading.\n- **Share of manufacturing output:** whether the sector’s share remains near or above the **7.32%** recorded in **2025**.\n- **Data revisions:** 2025 figures are preliminary estimates, so operators should treat the latest level and growth rate as subject to revision.\n\n## Why it matters to business\n\nFor businesses, transport engineering output is a practical signal for demand conditions across aviation, marine, logistics, maintenance, and regional industrial services. When the sector strengthens faster than total manufacturing, it can indicate improving workloads for firms tied to aircraft maintenance, ship repair, fleet support, engineering services, and specialised manufacturing supply chains.\n\nFor operators, the data can inform capacity planning, labour allocation, inventory decisions, and supplier readiness. For investors and planners, the relative performance against total manufacturing provides a useful benchmark: the supplied intelligence records **113.54%** 10-year growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, or **35.98 percentage points** of outperformance.\n\nThe business implication is not that all transport-linked companies will benefit equally. The aggregate output data does not identify firm-level exposure, contract margins, or the split between aviation and marine demand. It does, however, indicate that the broader transport engineering activity base has strengthened in a way that is relevant for commercial planning.\n\n## Transparency Panel\n\n| Item | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Confidence | High signal strength in the supplied intelligence; confidence tier: **established_pattern**; stability assessment: **stable_pattern**. |\n| Evidence base | Annual manufacturing output data for **Other Transport Equipment** and **Total Manufacturing**, 2016–2025, from the Economic Development Board table supplied through SingStat Table Builder. |\n| Data coverage | Annual data from **2016** to **2025**. Latest attached value for Other Transport Equipment: **33614.4** in **2025**; latest year-on-year growth: **31.49%**. |\n| Primary mechanism | Aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles. |\n| Trend classification | **recovering_expansion**. |\n| Relative performance | Supplied mandatory intelligence states **113.54%** 10-year growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, an outperformance of **35.98 percentage points**. |\n| Sector ranking | Supplied mandatory intelligence states **Precision Engineering ranked #3 out of 6** by 10-year growth. Article intelligence also records latest year-on-year growth rank of **#2 out of 6**. |\n| Manufacturing share | Supplied mandatory intelligence states share increased from **6.08%** in **2016** to **7.32%** in **2025**, a gain of **1.23 percentage points**. |\n| Known limitations | The attached source series is **Other Transport Equipment**. The supplied mandatory findings use the label **Precision Engineering**, creating a naming inconsistency that requires human editorial review before publication. The aggregate source series does not split aviation from marine activity. Year **2025** data are preliminary estimates. |\n| Proxy disclosure | Proxy disclosure is not required in the supplied package. The direct series used is **Other Transport Equipment**. |\n| Source notes | Figures include all manufacturing establishments. Industries are classified according to **SSIC 2025**. Valuation of output has been changed to basic price, and historical manufacturing output data have been revised accordingly from 1980. |\n| Human review status | Human review is required. Governance status indicates publication is not yet allowed. |\n\n## Sources\n\n1. **Singapore other transport equipment output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, “Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual”  \n   - Row: **Other Transport Equipment**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: From 2002, figures include establishments with less than 10 workers. Prior to 2002, figures refer to establishments engaging 10 or more workers. Data include all manufacturing establishments. Industries are classified according to **Singapore Standard Industrial Classification (SSIC) 2025**. Valuation of output has been changed to basic price. Historical data on manufacturing output has been revised accordingly from 1980. Year **2025** data are preliminary estimates.\n\n2. **Singapore total manufacturing output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, “Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual”  \n   - Row: **Total Manufacturing**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: From 2002, figures include establishments with less than 10 workers. Prior to 2002, figures refer to establishments engaging 10 or more workers. Data include all manufacturing establishments. Industries are classified according to **Singapore Standard Industrial Classification (SSIC) 2025**. Valuation of output has been changed to basic price. Historical data on manufacturing output has been revised accordingly from 1980. Year **2025** data are preliminary estimates.",
+      "markdown": "# What Transport Engineering Reveals About Singapore’s Aviation and Marine Recovery\n\nZNBW’s assessment is that Singapore’s transport engineering signal is best read as a recovery-linked industrial cycle rather than a standalone manufacturing movement. The available evidence points to aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles as the main business mechanisms behind the sector’s recent strength.\n\nThe strongest verified signal is that the attached transport-equipment output series reached **33614.4** in **2025**, with latest year-on-year growth of **31.49%**. Over the longer period, the supplied intelligence also shows the sector’s 10-year performance running ahead of total manufacturing, which makes the movement relevant for operators exposed to aerospace, marine engineering, transport engineering, and MRO demand.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- [The transport engineering signal is consistent with recovery in aviation and marine activity](#znbw-assessment), with confidence assessed as high but still subject to human editorial review.\n- [The data shows a stronger 10-year trajectory than total manufacturing](#what-the-data-shows): the supplied intelligence records **113.54%** growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, an outperformance of **35.98 percentage points**.\n- [Sector positioning is meaningful but not dominant](#what-the-historical-evidence-shows): the supplied mandatory intelligence states a **#3 out of 6** rank by 10-year growth, while the article intelligence also records a latest year-on-year growth rank of **#2 out of 6**.\n- [Manufacturing share has widened](#what-the-data-shows), increasing from **6.08%** in **2016** to **7.32%** in **2025**, a gain of **1.23 percentage points**.\n- [Businesses should watch whether recovery-linked demand persists](#what-operators-should-watch-next), especially in MRO, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles.\n\n## ZNBW Assessment\n\nZNBW assesses the transport engineering movement as a **recovering expansion** driven primarily by aviation and marine recovery mechanisms, including aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles. Confidence is **high**, supported by an established-pattern assessment in the supplied intelligence, but the article remains subject to **human review** because publication approval has not been granted and because the supplied mandatory findings use the label “Precision Engineering” while the attached source series is “Other Transport Equipment.”\n\n## What happened\n\nSingapore’s transport engineering-linked output strengthened materially in the latest data period. The attached source series, **Other Transport Equipment**, reached **33614.4** in **2025**, with latest year-on-year growth of **31.49%**.\n\nThat movement matters because the transport-equipment category is closely aligned with business cycles in aerospace, marine engineering, and maintenance-related activity. For companies exposed to aircraft servicing, fleet support, ship repair, marine systems, or related engineering capacity, the data points to a stronger operating backdrop than was visible during the earlier post-2020 period.\n\n[GRAPH: Singapore other transport equipment output trend, 2016–2025]\n\nThe annual path shows a decline into 2020, followed by a recovery phase from 2021 onward. Output rose from **13592.3** in 2020 to **14812.2** in 2021, **18147.8** in 2022, **21571.0** in 2023, **25563.6** in 2024, and **33614.4** in 2025.\n\n## What the data shows\n\nThe supplied article intelligence indicates that the transport-equipment signal has outpaced total manufacturing over the 10-year period. The mandatory intelligence states that **Precision Engineering grew 113.54% over 10 years versus 77.56% for total manufacturing, an outperformance of 35.98 percentage points**. In the attached source data, those same 10-year growth values correspond to the **Other Transport Equipment** row and the **Total Manufacturing** row.\n\n[GRAPH: Indexed 10-year output trend for other transport equipment and total manufacturing, 2016–2025]\n\nThis relative performance is commercially relevant because it shows that the sector did more than recover in line with the broader manufacturing base. Total manufacturing output rose from **258788.4** in 2016 to **459504.1** in 2025, while other transport equipment rose from **15741.5** to **33614.4** over the same period.\n\nThe manufacturing share change also points to a larger footprint within the manufacturing base. The supplied mandatory intelligence states that **Computer, Electronic & Optical Products’ manufacturing share increased from 6.08% in 2016 to 7.32% in 2025, a gain of 1.23 percentage points**. Using the attached transport-equipment series and total manufacturing series, this share movement is consistent with the reported values.\n\n## What explains the movement\n\nThe available evidence points to a transport engineering cycle shaped by aviation and marine recovery rather than by a single isolated driver. The article context identifies aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles as the relevant mechanisms.\n\nFor aerospace and MRO operators, stronger transport-equipment output can be consistent with higher aircraft servicing, maintenance, and engineering activity as air travel and fleet utilisation recover. For marine engineering and ship repair operators, the same category may reflect demand linked to vessel maintenance, offshore activity, and regional logistics requirements.\n\nThe data should not be interpreted as a detailed breakdown of aviation versus marine contribution. The source row is an aggregate manufacturing category, so it supports a sector-level signal rather than a full decomposition of sub-sector drivers.\n\n## What the historical evidence shows\n\nThe historical profile is consistent with a recovery followed by expansion. Output fell from **16690.5** in 2019 to **13592.3** in 2020, then moved upward across the subsequent periods in the attached annual data.\n\nThe supplied intelligence classifies the trend as **recovering_expansion**. It also assigns the pattern a high signal strength, with a confidence tier described as an **established_pattern** and a stability assessment described as a **stable_pattern**. The recurrence assessment is **partially_recurring**, which suggests the signal has repeated enough to be meaningful but should still be monitored rather than treated as permanent.\n\nSector ranking also supports a measured interpretation. The mandatory intelligence states that **Precision Engineering ranked #3 out of 6 major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth**. The article intelligence further records a latest year-on-year growth rank of **#2 out of 6**. This places the sector among stronger manufacturing performers, but not as the top-ranked 10-year growth category.\n\n## What operators should watch next\n\nOperators should watch whether the latest strength translates into durable order flow, capacity utilisation, and maintenance demand. The most relevant watch items are:\n\n- **Aviation maintenance demand:** whether aircraft servicing and MRO workloads remain supportive.\n- **Marine engineering and ship repair activity:** whether fleet maintenance and offshore-related demand continue to support output.\n- **Manufacturing-relative performance:** whether the sector continues to outpace total manufacturing after the strong 2025 year-on-year reading.\n- **Share of manufacturing output:** whether the sector’s share remains near or above the **7.32%** recorded in **2025**.\n- **Data revisions:** 2025 figures are preliminary estimates, so operators should treat the latest level and growth rate as subject to revision.\n\n## Why it matters to business\n\nFor businesses, transport engineering output is a practical signal for demand conditions across aviation, marine, logistics, maintenance, and regional industrial services. When the sector strengthens faster than total manufacturing, it can indicate improving workloads for firms tied to aircraft maintenance, ship repair, fleet support, engineering services, and specialised manufacturing supply chains.\n\nFor operators, the data can inform capacity planning, labour allocation, inventory decisions, and supplier readiness. For investors and planners, the relative performance against total manufacturing provides a useful benchmark: the supplied intelligence records **113.54%** 10-year growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, or **35.98 percentage points** of outperformance.\n\nThe business implication is not that all transport-linked companies will benefit equally. The aggregate output data does not identify firm-level exposure, contract margins, or the split between aviation and marine demand. It does, however, indicate that the broader transport engineering activity base has strengthened in a way that is relevant for commercial planning.\n\n## Transparency Panel\n\n| Item | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Confidence | High signal strength in the supplied intelligence; confidence tier: **established_pattern**; stability assessment: **stable_pattern**. |\n| Evidence base | Annual manufacturing output data for **Other Transport Equipment** and **Total Manufacturing**, 2016–2025, from the Economic Development Board table supplied through SingStat Table Builder. |\n| Data coverage | Annual data from **2016** to **2025**. Latest attached value for Other Transport Equipment: **33614.4** in **2025**; latest year-on-year growth: **31.49%**. |\n| Primary mechanism | Aviation recovery, aircraft maintenance, marine engineering, ship repair, and fleet maintenance cycles. |\n| Trend classification | **recovering_expansion**. |\n| Relative performance | Supplied mandatory intelligence states **113.54%** 10-year growth versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing, an outperformance of