-      "markdown": "# Singapore Refined Petroleum Output Signals Energy-Cycle Exposure, but Momentum Weakened in 2025\n\nZNBW assesses Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing data as a useful signal of energy-cycle exposure rather than a standalone indicator of domestic demand. The movement points to sensitivity around fuel demand, travel activity, refining margins, trade flows, and operational factors in the refining complex.\n\nThe strongest verified near-term data point is that 2025 output reached **38344** million dollars, with latest year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**. That places the sector in a weaker momentum position even though the longer-period series remains above its 2016 level.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- [The main signal](#znbw-assessment) is cyclical exposure: refined petroleum output appears tied to global energy demand and refining-cycle conditions rather than a simple local production trend.\n- [The data shows](#what-the-data-shows) 10-year growth of **43.11%**, below total manufacturing growth of **77.56%**, implying relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points**.\n- [Sector ranking matters](#what-the-historical-evidence-shows): the supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6** major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth, requiring editorial review because the source series used here is refined petroleum products.\n- [Operators should watch](#what-operators-should-watch-next) fuel demand, travel recovery, refining margins, and refinery operating conditions as practical indicators for near-term exposure.\n\n## ZNBW Assessment\n\nThe available evidence suggests that Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing output is primarily being shaped by the **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle** mechanism. Confidence is **medium**, with the signal classified as an **emerging pattern** and a volatile pattern in the supplied assessment. The business relevance is strongest for petroleum, refining, energy, logistics, and trade-exposed operators. The main limitation is that the supplied mandatory findings refer to **Precision Engineering**, while the source series and graph package refer to **Refined Petroleum Products**; this label inconsistency requires human editorial review before publication.\n\n## What Happened\n\nSingapore’s refined petroleum products output weakened in the latest year after a period marked by large swings. The latest available annual value for 2025 is **38344** million dollars, with year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**.\n\nThis should be interpreted as a momentum warning rather than as evidence of structural collapse. The 10-year series remains higher than in 2016, but the pattern is volatile and appears consistent with exposure to global fuel demand, refining economics, and operating-cycle factors.\n\n[GRAPH: Singapore refined petroleum products output, 2016-2025]\n\n## What the Data Shows\n\nThe supplied intelligence records 10-year growth of **43.11%** for the relevant sector series versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing. That implies relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points** compared with total manufacturing.\n\nThis relative underperformance is important because total manufacturing expanded more strongly over the period. In business terms, refined petroleum-related manufacturing did not keep pace with the broader manufacturing base, even though the level of output remained above its 2016 value.\n\n[GRAPH: Indexed trend comparison of refined petroleum products output versus total manufacturing, 2016-2025]\n\nThe supplied manufacturing-share finding states that **Precision Engineering's manufacturing share increased from 10.35% in 2016 to 8.34% in 2025, a gain of -2.01 percentage points**. Numerically, a move from **10.35%** to **8.34%** with a **-2.01** percentage-point change indicates a decline in share; the wording of “increased” and “gain” should therefore be treated as a data-label or drafting issue requiring review.\n\n## What Explains the Movement\n\nThe primary mechanism identified in the article package is the **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle**. That means annual output movements are likely to be associated with shifts in fuel demand, travel-related consumption, refining margins, logistics activity, and refinery operating conditions.\n\nFor Singapore, this matters because refined petroleum output is connected to external demand channels. A weaker output year can reflect changes in regional fuel consumption, trade flows, maintenance cycles, pricing conditions, or refining margins. The supplied package does not provide margin, volume, utilisation, or plant-level data, so the evidence should be read as a manufacturing-output signal rather than a complete explanation of refinery economics.\n\n## What the Historical Evidence Shows\n\nThe historical output series shows a volatile profile. Refined petroleum products output was **26793.8** million dollars in 2016, rose through 2018, fell sharply in 2020, rebounded in 2021 and 2022, and then eased from the 2022 high through 2025.\n\nThe supplied mandatory sector-ranking finding states that **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6 major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth**. This **#4 out of 6** ranking places the labelled sector in the middle-to-lower part of the peer group on a 10-year growth basis. However, because the article package’s source series is **Refined Petroleum Products**, this ranking label should be verified by a human editor before final publication.\n\nThe latest momentum ranking in the supplied article intelligence also indicates a weak current-year position: the relevant sector is ranked **6** out of **6** by latest year-on-year growth. That is consistent with the 2025 year-on-year contraction of **-9.02%**.\n\n## What Operators Should Watch Next\n\nBusinesses exposed to petroleum, refining, energy, logistics, and related trade flows should monitor several practical indicators:\n\n1. **Fuel demand and travel activity** — especially changes that affect jet fuel, marine fuel, and transport-linked demand.\n2. **Refining-cycle conditions** — including margin pressure and product-market balance, where available from external operating data.\n3. **Refinery operations and maintenance cycles** — because operational downtime can affect annual manufacturing output.\n4. **Relative manufacturing performance** — because the sector’s 10-year growth of **43.11%** lagged total manufacturing growth of **77.56%** by **-34.45 percentage points**.\n5. **Share of manufacturing output** — the supplied share values move from **10.35%** in 2016 to **8.34%** in 2025, a **-2.01** percentage-point change, indicating a reduced share on the numbers provided.\n\n## Why It Matters to Business\n\nFor operators, the data points to exposure rather than certainty. Petroleum-related manufacturing movements can affect procurement planning, shipping demand, storage utilisation, working-capital assumptions, and energy-linked industrial activity.\n\nFor investors and planners, the key issue is that refined petroleum output can move differently from broader manufacturing. Total manufacturing grew **77.56%** over the 10-year period, while the relevant sector series grew **43.11%**, producing relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points**. That gap suggests the sector should not be treated as a simple proxy for overall manufacturing strength.\n\nFor logistics and trade-exposed businesses, weaker petroleum output momentum in 2025 may signal softer throughput conditions or operating-cycle effects. The available data does not identify the exact driver, but it is consistent with the need to watch global fuel demand, travel recovery, refining margins, and refinery operations.\n\n## Transparency Panel\n\n| Item | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Confidence | Medium signal strength; supplied confidence score **0.933**; confidence tier: **emerging pattern**. |\n| Evidence base | Annual manufacturing output data for refined petroleum products and total manufacturing, with supporting comparison series for chemicals and other transport equipment. |\n| Data coverage | Annual data from **2016 to 2025**. The latest period is **2025**. |\n| Primary mechanism | **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle**, covering fuel demand, travel activity, refining margins, logistics conditions, and operational factors. |\n| Trend classification | **Declining**, based on the supplied article intelligence. |\n| Latest momentum | In **2025**, output reached **38344** million dollars with latest year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**. |\n| Relative performance | The supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering grew 43.11% over 10 years versus 77.56% for total manufacturing, an outperformance of -34.45 percentage points**. The numerical comparison is used, but the sector label requires review because the source row is refined petroleum products. |\n| Sector ranking | The supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6** major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth. This must be checked against the refined petroleum source label before publication. |\n| Manufacturing share | The supplied finding states share moved from **10.35%** in **2016** to **8.34%** in **2025**, a **-2.01** percentage-point change. Numerically, this is a decline despite the supplied wording of “increased” and “gain.” |\n| Known limitations | 2025 data are preliminary estimates. The package contains a label inconsistency: mandatory findings refer to Precision Engineering, while the data source and graph package refer to Refined Petroleum Products. No plant-level utilisation, refining-margin, demand-volume, or operational-maintenance data are supplied. |\n| Proxy disclosure | No proxy disclosure is required; the supplied direct series is **Refined Petroleum Products**. |\n| Source notes | Source table is EDB manufacturing output by industry via SingStat TableBuilder. Figures are in million dollars and classified according to SSIC 2025. |\n| Human review status | Human review is required. Governance indicates publication is not allowed without review. |\n\n## Sources\n\n1. **Singapore refined petroleum products output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Refined Petroleum Products**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: Figures include all manufacturing establishments; industries classified according to SSIC 2025; valuation changed to basic price; historical data revised from 1980; **2025 data are preliminary estimates**.\n\n2. **Singapore total manufacturing output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Total Manufacturing**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: Same table methodology and preliminary 2025 status apply.\n\n3. **Singapore chemicals and chemical products output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Chemicals & Chemical Products**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**.\n\n4. **Singapore other transport equipment output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Other Transport Equipment**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**.",
+      "markdown": "# Singapore Refined Petroleum Output Signals Energy-Cycle Exposure, but Momentum Weakened in 2025\n\nZNBW assesses Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing data as a useful signal of energy-cycle exposure rather than a standalone indicator of domestic demand. The movement points to sensitivity around fuel demand, travel activity, refining margins, trade flows, and operational factors in the refining complex.\n\nThe strongest verified near-term data point is that 2025 output reached **38344** million dollars, with latest year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**. That places the sector in a weaker momentum position even though the longer-period series remains above its 2016 level.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- [The main signal](#znbw-assessment) is cyclical exposure: refined petroleum output appears tied to global energy demand and refining-cycle conditions rather than a simple local production trend.\n- [The data shows](#what-the-data-shows) 10-year growth of **43.11%**, below total manufacturing growth of **77.56%**, implying relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points**.\n- [Sector ranking matters](#what-the-historical-evidence-shows): the supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6** major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth, requiring editorial review because the source series used here is refined petroleum products.\n- [Operators should watch](#what-operators-should-watch-next) fuel demand, travel recovery, refining margins, and refinery operating conditions as practical indicators for near-term exposure.\n\n## ZNBW Assessment\n\nThe available evidence suggests that Singapore’s refined petroleum manufacturing output is primarily being shaped by the **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle** mechanism. Confidence is **medium**, with the signal classified as an **emerging pattern** and a volatile pattern in the supplied assessment. The business relevance is strongest for petroleum, refining, energy, logistics, and trade-exposed operators. The main limitation is that the supplied mandatory findings refer to **Precision Engineering**, while the source series and graph package refer to **Refined Petroleum Products**; this label inconsistency requires human editorial review before publication.\n\n## What Happened\n\nSingapore’s refined petroleum products output weakened in the latest year after a period marked by large swings. The latest available annual value for 2025 is **38344** million dollars, with year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**.\n\nThis should be interpreted as a momentum warning rather than as evidence of structural collapse. The 10-year series remains higher than in 2016, but the pattern is volatile and appears consistent with exposure to global fuel demand, refining economics, and operating-cycle factors.\n\n[GRAPH: Singapore refined petroleum products output, 2016-2025]\n\n## What the Data Shows\n\nThe supplied intelligence records 10-year growth of **43.11%** for the relevant sector series versus **77.56%** for total manufacturing. That implies relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points** compared with total manufacturing.\n\nThis relative underperformance is important because total manufacturing expanded more strongly over the period. In business terms, refined petroleum-related manufacturing did not keep pace with the broader manufacturing base, even though the level of output remained above its 2016 value.\n\n[GRAPH: Indexed trend comparison of refined petroleum products output versus total manufacturing, 2016-2025]\n\nThe supplied manufacturing-share finding states that **Computer, Electronic & Optical Products' manufacturing share increased from 10.35% in 2016 to 8.34% in 2025, a gain of -2.01 percentage points**. Numerically, a move from **10.35%** to **8.34%** with a **-2.01** percentage-point change indicates a decline in share; the wording of “increased” and “gain” should therefore be treated as a data-label or drafting issue requiring review.\n\n## What Explains the Movement\n\nThe primary mechanism identified in the article package is the **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle**. That means annual output movements are likely to be associated with shifts in fuel demand, travel-related consumption, refining margins, logistics activity, and refinery operating conditions.\n\nFor Singapore, this matters because refined petroleum output is connected to external demand channels. A weaker output year can reflect changes in regional fuel consumption, trade flows, maintenance cycles, pricing conditions, or refining margins. The supplied package does not provide margin, volume, utilisation, or plant-level data, so the evidence should be read as a manufacturing-output signal rather than a complete explanation of refinery economics.\n\n## What the Historical Evidence Shows\n\nThe historical output series shows a volatile profile. Refined petroleum products output was **26793.8** million dollars in 2016, rose through 2018, fell sharply in 2020, rebounded in 2021 and 2022, and then eased from the 2022 high through 2025.\n\nThe supplied mandatory sector-ranking finding states that **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6 major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth**. This **#4 out of 6** ranking places the labelled sector in the middle-to-lower part of the peer group on a 10-year growth basis. However, because the article package’s source series is **Refined Petroleum Products**, this ranking label should be verified by a human editor before final publication.\n\nThe latest momentum ranking in the supplied article intelligence also indicates a weak current-year position: the relevant sector is ranked **6** out of **6** by latest year-on-year growth. That is consistent with the 2025 year-on-year contraction of **-9.02%**.\n\n## What Operators Should Watch Next\n\nBusinesses exposed to petroleum, refining, energy, logistics, and related trade flows should monitor several practical indicators:\n\n1. **Fuel demand and travel activity** — especially changes that affect jet fuel, marine fuel, and transport-linked demand.\n2. **Refining-cycle conditions** — including margin pressure and product-market balance, where available from external operating data.\n3. **Refinery operations and maintenance cycles** — because operational downtime can affect annual manufacturing output.\n4. **Relative manufacturing performance** — because the sector’s 10-year growth of **43.11%** lagged total manufacturing growth of **77.56%** by **-34.45 percentage points**.\n5. **Share of manufacturing output** — the supplied share values move from **10.35%** in 2016 to **8.34%** in 2025, a **-2.01** percentage-point change, indicating a reduced share on the numbers provided.\n\n## Why It Matters to Business\n\nFor operators, the data points to exposure rather than certainty. Petroleum-related manufacturing movements can affect procurement planning, shipping demand, storage utilisation, working-capital assumptions, and energy-linked industrial activity.\n\nFor investors and planners, the key issue is that refined petroleum output can move differently from broader manufacturing. Total manufacturing grew **77.56%** over the 10-year period, while the relevant sector series grew **43.11%**, producing relative performance of **-34.45 percentage points**. That gap suggests the sector should not be treated as a simple proxy for overall manufacturing strength.\n\nFor logistics and trade-exposed businesses, weaker petroleum output momentum in 2025 may signal softer throughput conditions or operating-cycle effects. The available data does not identify the exact driver, but it is consistent with the need to watch global fuel demand, travel recovery, refining margins, and refinery operations.\n\n## Transparency Panel\n\n| Item | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Confidence | Medium signal strength; supplied confidence score **0.933**; confidence tier: **emerging pattern**. |\n| Evidence base | Annual manufacturing output data for refined petroleum products and total manufacturing, with supporting comparison series for chemicals and other transport equipment. |\n| Data coverage | Annual data from **2016 to 2025**. The latest period is **2025**. |\n| Primary mechanism | **Energy Demand / Refining Cycle**, covering fuel demand, travel activity, refining margins, logistics conditions, and operational factors. |\n| Trend classification | **Declining**, based on the supplied article intelligence. |\n| Latest momentum | In **2025**, output reached **38344** million dollars with latest year-on-year growth of **-9.02%**. |\n| Relative performance | The supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering grew 43.11% over 10 years versus 77.56% for total manufacturing, an outperformance of -34.45 percentage points**. The numerical comparison is used, but the sector label requires review because the source row is refined petroleum products. |\n| Sector ranking | The supplied mandatory finding states **Precision Engineering ranked #4 out of 6** major manufacturing sectors by 10-year growth. This must be checked against the refined petroleum source label before publication. |\n| Manufacturing share | The supplied finding states share moved from **10.35%** in **2016** to **8.34%** in **2025**, a **-2.01** percentage-point change. Numerically, this is a decline despite the supplied wording of “increased” and “gain.” |\n| Known limitations | 2025 data are preliminary estimates. The package contains a label inconsistency: mandatory findings refer to Precision Engineering, while the data source and graph package refer to Refined Petroleum Products. No plant-level utilisation, refining-margin, demand-volume, or operational-maintenance data are supplied. |\n| Proxy disclosure | No proxy disclosure is required; the supplied direct series is **Refined Petroleum Products**. |\n| Source notes | Source table is EDB manufacturing output by industry via SingStat TableBuilder. Figures are in million dollars and classified according to SSIC 2025. |\n| Human review status | Human review is required. Governance indicates publication is not allowed without review. |\n\n## Sources\n\n1. **Singapore refined petroleum products output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Refined Petroleum Products**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: Figures include all manufacturing establishments; industries classified according to SSIC 2025; valuation changed to basic price; historical data revised from 1980; **2025 data are preliminary estimates**.\n\n2. **Singapore total manufacturing output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Total Manufacturing**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**  \n   - Notes: Same table methodology and preliminary 2025 status apply.\n\n3. **Singapore chemicals and chemical products output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Chemicals & Chemical Products**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**.\n\n4. **Singapore other transport equipment output**  \n   - Source: Economic Development Board  \n   - Table: **M354851**, *Manufacturing Output In Manufacturing By Industry, Annual*  \n   - Row: **Other Transport Equipment**  \n   - URL: https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/api/table/tabledata/M354851  \n   - Data last updated: **11/05/2026**  \n   - Date generated: **14/06/2026**.",
